    "result_text": "The build passed. There's only one test class in the project (`AcceptanceTest`, plus its non-test base/helpers), and the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented membership level 4 gated on tenure of more than 365 days.\n\n**`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a domain method that expresses tenure as elapsed days since registration (returns 0 when the registration date is absent or hasn't yet elapsed), reusing the entity's existing `registrationDate` field:\n```java\npublic long tenureInDays(LocalDate asOf) {\n    if (this.registrationDate == null || asOf == null) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    return Math.max(0, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(this.registrationDate, asOf));\n}\n```\n\n**`MembershipLevelEvaluator.java`** \u2014 added a fourth qualifying attribute (+1 when `tenureInDays > 365`), raised the cap `MAX_LEVEL` from 3 to 4, and injected a `Clock` (defaulting to system zone) so \"today\" stays testable and deterministic:\n```java\nif (hasQualifyingTenure(owner)) {\n    level++;\n}\n...\nprivate boolean hasQualifyingTenure(Owner owner) {\n    return owner.tenureInDays(LocalDate.now(clock)) > TENURE_THRESHOLD_DAYS;\n}\n```\n\n**Why a new owner never exceeds level 3:** a newly created owner's `registrationDate` defaults to the current date, so its tenure is 0 (\u2264 365), leaving only the email and no-namesake attributes \u2014 max level 3. Level 4 becomes reachable only once tenure passes the threshold. This keeps each responsibility in its place: tenure math lives on the `Owner` domain object (mirroring the existing `AgeBand` date logic), and the level policy stays in the evaluator.\n\nThe acceptance test (`coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3`) and the full suite pass: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
